        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Nb4hvwpfppr6BDCtpLXxxT",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/backpressure.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Backpressure in Streaming Pipelines\\ndescription: Mechanism by which a slow downstream consumer signals an upstream producer to reduce its emission rate, preventing unbounded buffer growth.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/backpressure\\n  - concept/flow-control\\n  - pattern/streaming\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single source providing clear definition and implementation patterns; no corroboration yet.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nBackpressure is a mechanism used in streaming pipelines to coordinate between producers and consumers when processing rates differ. Rather than allowing an unbounded buffer to grow between a fast upstream producer and a slow downstream consumer (which eventually exhausts memory), backpressure signals the producer to reduce its emission rate.\\n\\n## How Backpressure Works\\n\\nA slow downstream consumer uses backpressure to communicate upstream: \\u0022I cannot keep up with your current emission rate; please slow down.\\u0022 This prevents the system from accumulating an ever-growing queue of unprocessed items.\\n\\n## Common Implementation Patterns\\n\\n### Bounded Queues\\nThe producer and consumer are connected by a queue with a fixed maximum size. Once the queue fills, the producer is blocked (or paused) until the consumer drains items, freeing up space.\\n\\n### Credit-Based Flow Control\\nThe consumer explicitly grants the producer permission to send a certain number of items (a \\u0022credit\\u0022). When the producer exhausts its credit, it must wait for the consumer to grant additional credits before sending more items. This gives the consumer fine-grained control over the rate of arrival.\\n\\n### Reactive-Pull Models\\nRather than the producer pushing items to the consumer, the consumer requests items on its own schedule. The consumer pulls items only when it is ready to process them, naturally preventing overload.\\n\\n## Purpose and Benefits\\n\\n- **Memory safety:** Prevents unbounded buffer growth and out-of-memory failures\\n- **Rate matching:** Allows systems to operate safely even when upstream and downstream rates differ significantly\\n- **Automatic regulation:** The mechanism can adapt to changing consumer load without manual tuning\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Streaming pipeline flow control mechanisms (eval://catalog-description-specificity/sample-04)\\n\u0022}"
        },
